{
  "dom_id": "step-q146407",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=400$ from a population where $32%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,400.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{400}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=32%=0.32,\\,n=400.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(400,0.32)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =400\\cdot 0.32 \\\\  & =128 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =400\\cdot 0.32\\cdot (1-0.32) \\\\  & =87.04\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
